Milestone 2: ThoughtObject IR

Everything should operate on the same substrate.

from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from typing import Any
from uuid import uuid4
@dataclass
class ThoughtObject:
    id: str = field(default_factory=lambda: str(uuid4()))
    content: Any = None
    relations: dict[str, list[str]] = field(default_factory=dict)
    invariants: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    provenance: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    confidence: float = 0.0
    uncertainty: float = 1.0
    route: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    rejected_routes: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    losses: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    metadata: dict[str, Any] = field(default_factory=dict)
    def transform(self, operator: str, result: Any):
        return ThoughtObject(
            content=result,
            relations=self.relations.copy(),
            invariants=self.invariants.copy(),
            provenance=self.provenance + [self.id],
            confidence=self.confidence,
            uncertainty=self.uncertainty,
            route=self.route + [operator],
            rejected_routes=self.rejected_routes.copy(),
            losses=self.losses.copy(),
            metadata=self.metadata.copy(),
        )

That sounds mundane.

It is not.

Once this exists:

\text{Hammer}(T)
\rightarrow
\text{Metamap}(T)
\rightarrow
\text{Kintsugi}(T)

without translating the object back through human prose between every operation.

That’s a genuine architecture milestone.

