Milestone 3: benchmark before more invention

Start with five problem families, not fifty.

B1 — Hidden relation

Give several apparently unrelated examples containing a shared structural invariant.

Measure whether the system discovers it.

B2 — Representation escape

Give a problem designed to be hard in its native framing but easy after representation change.

Measure:

\text{time-to-useful-reframe}

B3 — Failed-path salvage

Give a deliberately incorrect attempted solution containing useful pieces.

Measure value recovered rather than merely detecting failure.

B4 — Cross-domain transfer

Teach a mechanism in domain A.

Ask it to deploy the invariant in unfamiliar domain B.

B5 — Branch quality

Give an underspecified problem requiring exploration.

Measure useful-diverse branches versus redundant branches.

That becomes:

bench/
├── cases/
│   ├── hidden_relation.jsonl
│   ├── representation_escape.jsonl
│   ├── failure_salvage.jsonl
│   ├── cross_domain_transfer.jsonl
│   └── branch_quality.jsonl
├── evaluators/
├── baselines/
├── pantheon/
└── results/

And every case gets run under:

C_0=\text{baseline}

C_1=\text{full Pantheon}

Then later:

C_{-i}=\text{Pantheon without operator }i

That immediately unlocks the ablation milestone.

⸻

The scorecard

Do not reduce everything to “answer correct / incorrect.”

Use:

Q=
w_aA+
w_nN+
w_tT+
w_rR+
w_cC
-
w_hH
-
w_kK

Where:

* A: correctness
* N: novelty/useful delta
* T: transferability
* R: recoverability
* C: compression
* H: hallucinated structure
* K: reasoning/resource cost

The killer metric is:

\boxed{
\text{Pantheon Gain}
=
\frac{Q_P-Q_B}{\text{Cost}_P-\text{Cost}_B}
}

Because a framework that improves answers by 2% while consuming 20× more reasoning isn’t automatically winning.

